Need think through exact behavior with LTL. Requirements:
1 inputs bool.
2 Compute desired direction: 0 if Stop TRUE or ForwardButton = ReverseButton; +1 if only ForwardButton TRUE; -1 if only ReverseButton TRUE.
3 If desired direction nonzero and opposite to previous scan's active direction (-1 vs +1), force both outputs FALSE for this one intermediate stop scan (clearing direction memory to 0).
4 Otherwise output desired direction and store it.

Initial previous_direction=0, outputs false.

Implementation can be simple:
VAR_INPUT
 ForwardButton: BOOL;
 ReverseButton: BOOL;
 Stop: BOOL;
END_VAR
VAR_OUTPUT
 Forward: BOOL;
 Reverse: BOOL;
END_VAR
VAR
 previous_direction: INT := 0;
 desired_direction: INT;
END_VAR
Implementation:
desired_direction := 0;
IF Stop OR (ForwardButton = ReverseButton) THEN
    desired_direction := 0;
ELSIF ForwardButton THEN
    desired_direction := 1;
ELSIF ReverseButton THEN
    desired_direction := -1;
END_IF

IF (desired_direction <> 0) AND (previous_direction <> 0) AND (desired_direction <> previous_direction) THEN
    Forward := FALSE;
    Reverse := FALSE;
    previous_direction := 0;
ELSE
    Forward := (desired_direction = 1);
    Reverse := (desired_direction = -1);
    previous_direction := desired_direction;
END_IF

Need ensure qForward/qReverse previous observed outputs? Requirements and LTL use qForward/qReverse from previous scan outputs; but state previous_direction enough? Let's examine LTL P3/P4: G((fwdOnly & qReverse) -> (!Forward & !Reverse)). qReverse is previous observed output Reverse, initial false. P5: G((fwdOnly & !qReverse) -> Forward). With our previous_direction may differ from previous outputs when reversal neutral scan: after reversal neutral, previous_direction=0 but qReverse previous scan was true (because before neutral, reverse output was true). Next scan fwdOnly persists. According to our code: desired=+1, previous_direction=0, no opposite, outputs Forward immediately. But LTL P3: fwdOnly & qReverse (qReverse = previous observed output) -> neutral. At next scan after neutral, qReverse is previous observed output = false (because neutral scan set Reverse false). So P3 doesn't demand another neutral. P5: fwdOnly & !qReverse -> Forward. At next scan qReverse false, so Forward true. So okay. What about if desired opposite and previous_direction from memory but qReverse false due to some previous neutral? Scenario: enter forward, then Stop? previous_direction cleared. If reverse output was false. So okay. But consider ambiguous input after reverse, outputs neutral and previous_direction=0. qReverse previous output true. Then fwdOnly next scan? qReverse true from prior scan, LTL P3 says must neutral because fwdOnly & qReverse. Our code previous_direction=0 would output Forward immediately. This violates P3 if qReverse remains true even though previous_direction cleared due to ambiguous/stop. Let's simulate: scan0 ReverseButton only -> Reverse true, previous_direction=-1. scan1 Stop true? desired 0, outputs false, previous_direction=0. qReverse at scan2 = O[scan1].Reverse = false. Wait qReverse is previous observed output after scan1, false. So not true. To have qReverse true at scan2, scan1 output must Reverse true. But if scan1 had Stop/ambiguous, output false. So qReverse cannot be true after a neutral. Only if previous scan output Reverse true, which means previous_direction was -1 and no interlock. Then if current fwdOnly, previous_direction=-1, our code inserts neutral. Good. So previous_direction equivalent to qReverse/qForward? Except both false when stopped. Yes.

